  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 251 AM CDT Tue Sep 29 2026\n\nCurrently have an upper low digging across Southern CA with Polo \nstill down over central Baja. Meanwhile over the Plains, \nsouthwest flow aloft is place. At the surface, a broad area of \nhigh pressure extends from the Great Lakes down through the \nTennessee Valley. \n\nUpper energy, that that is currently situated over NM, will \nquickly lift northeast this morning and will be moving out \nacross the TX Panhandle around sunrise. In response, low level \njet will strengthen and veer allowing 850mb moisture transport \nto quickly increase. This will result in some showers and iso \nstorms to move into south central KS after sunrise and becoming\nmore widespread after 15z through the afternoon hours. BY 00z \nthis area of precip will be lifting off to the northeast with \nthe bulk of the convection tonight focused along the 850mb \ntrough which will be laid out from eastern Nebraska into western\nKS, which will lead to much of the area staying dry overnight. \n\nPowerful upper low will be digging across the Desert Southwest Wed \nmorning with a strong upper jet max lifting through the TX Panhandle \nand into southwest KS. At the same time, what is left of Polo will \nbe quickly lifting across eastern NM and into eastern CO/sw KS. By \nWed afternoon, there is good model agreement in a sfc low situated \nover western KS/eastern CO with a strong jet max and associated\ndry slot also nosing into west/central KS. The most widespread \nprecip during the day Wed looks to be northwest of the sfc low \nin the wrap around region and in the warm sector where moisture \ntransport will be maximized. So where the sfc low ends up being \nlocated will be very important. Currently have a lean to the sfc\nlow being further west than a few of the models are indicating,\npossibly over eastern CO. This would also lead to the dry slot \nfurther west and possibly a wetter scenario for our forecast \narea. There also looks to be some sfc based severe storm \npotential Wed afternoon/early evening east of the sfc low as a \ndryline/sfc trough pushes east. With strong jet, shear will not \nbe an issue so will be dependent on if we can get some clearing\neast of the sfc low. Where this occurs will be highly reliant \non where the sfc low ends up. \n\nA large area of heavy rain will setup on Wed night into Thu \nmorning as right entrance region of upper jet interacts with \ntropical like airmass in place. The most likely area to see the \nheavy rain looks to be from northeast TX through eastern OK and \npossibly into far eastern KS. System will start to quickly shear\nout as it slides east on Thu afternoon into Thu night and what \nis left of it by Fri will extend from central MO down into \nsouth central TX with the better rainfall chances southeast of \nthe remnant upper trough. So by Fri afternoon, most of the \nrainfall will be out of our forecast area with precip expected \nto remain out through the remainder of the forecast.",
